1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,899
On the battlefield, among the wounded who could have

2
00:00:03,899 --> 00:00:06,184
been saved, the leading cause of death is

3
00:00:06,184 --> 00:00:07,187
hemorrhagic shock.

4
00:00:07,187 --> 00:00:08,302
Shock from bleeding.

5
00:00:08,302 --> 00:00:10,922
That's how one Army regimental surgeon puts it.

6
00:00:11,372 --> 00:00:12,505
The answer is blood.

7
00:00:12,505 --> 00:00:14,206
But blood has to be kept cold.

8
00:00:14,206 --> 00:00:15,792
It has to be the right type.

9
00:00:15,792 --> 00:00:17,889
And far from a hospital, it runs out.

10
00:00:18,339 --> 00:00:20,395
So the military went back to an old idea.

11
00:00:20,395 --> 00:00:22,903
If you can't carry enough blood, bring the donors.

12
00:00:23,353 --> 00:00:26,412
On a remote island in the Indian Ocean, volunteers

13
00:00:26,412 --> 00:00:28,063
are screened ahead of time.

14
00:00:28,063 --> 00:00:31,184
If someone is badly hurt, the blood bank is already

15
00:00:31,184 --> 00:00:32,897
on the base, walking around.

16
00:00:33,497 --> 00:00:36,541
This is how whole blood, and the walking blood bank,

17
00:00:36,541 --> 00:00:37,595
keep people alive.

18
00:00:40,195 --> 00:00:42,023
Start with what blood actually is.

19
00:00:42,473 --> 00:00:45,559
More than half of it is plasma, a liquid of water,

20
00:00:45,559 --> 00:00:46,670
salts and protein.

21
00:00:46,670 --> 00:00:48,460
Red blood cells carry oxygen.

22
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:50,559
White blood cells fight infection.

23
00:00:50,559 --> 00:00:52,411
And platelets help blood clot.

24
00:00:52,861 --> 00:00:55,268
Whole blood is all of it, together, in one bag.

25
00:00:55,718 --> 00:00:58,771
But most transfusions don't use it that way.

26
00:00:58,771 --> 00:01:02,657
Donated blood is more often given as its separate parts.

27
00:01:02,657 --> 00:01:04,670
Red cells. Plasma. Platelets.

28
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:06,479
And there's a catch.

29
00:01:06,479 --> 00:01:07,907
Blood comes in types.

30
00:01:08,357 --> 00:01:11,751
In 1901, Karl Landsteiner reported that human blood

31
00:01:11,751 --> 00:01:13,614
falls into different groups.

32
00:01:13,614 --> 00:01:16,875
Mix the wrong ones, and red cells clump together.

33
00:01:16,875 --> 00:01:20,735
That explained why early transfusions so often went wrong.

34
00:01:20,735 --> 00:01:22,731
Today we know four main types.

35
00:01:22,731 --> 00:01:23,796
A, B, AB, and O.

36
00:01:23,796 --> 00:01:26,924
And blood is either Rh-positive or Rh-negative.

37
00:01:27,374 --> 00:01:29,332
The clumping comes from antibodies.

38
00:01:29,332 --> 00:01:31,682
An Army blood bank director put it simply.

39
00:01:32,132 --> 00:01:33,772
Everyone has antibodies.

40
00:01:33,772 --> 00:01:36,780
They are naturally occurring, and can attach

41
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:39,309
themselves to transfused blood cells.

42
00:01:39,759 --> 00:01:42,960
Type O red cells are already considered universal.

43
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:46,226
But type O donors can carry antibodies against type

44
00:01:46,226 --> 00:01:47,442
A and type B blood.

45
00:01:47,442 --> 00:01:50,643
So the military tests type O donors, and keeps the

46
00:01:50,643 --> 00:01:52,308
ones whose levels are low.

47
00:01:52,758 --> 00:01:53,869
Below 1 to 256.

48
00:01:53,869 --> 00:01:55,793
That's called low titer O.

49
00:01:55,793 --> 00:01:59,347
The Army says it can be given to anyone in need,

50
00:01:59,347 --> 00:02:01,642
regardless of their blood type.

51
00:02:01,642 --> 00:02:05,343
Research cited by the Army found that at those low

52
00:02:05,343 --> 00:02:09,045
levels, the risk of a severe reaction in a patient

53
00:02:09,045 --> 00:02:12,302
of another blood type is low, or negligible.

54
00:02:13,102 --> 00:02:15,594
The idea of a blood bank was born in a war.

55
00:02:16,044 --> 00:02:19,210
In 1917, an American Army doctor named Oswald

56
00:02:19,210 --> 00:02:22,727
Robertson set up the first blood depot, in France.

57
00:02:22,727 --> 00:02:25,541
It was an ice chest, stocked with blood.

58
00:02:25,991 --> 00:02:29,007
His donors were usually lightly wounded soldiers,

59
00:02:29,007 --> 00:02:30,115
with type O blood.

60
00:02:30,115 --> 00:02:31,654
Each gave about two cups.

61
00:02:31,654 --> 00:02:34,670
The blood was mixed with sodium citrate, bottled,

62
00:02:34,670 --> 00:02:37,932
and packed in ammunition boxes, with sawdust and ice.

63
00:02:37,932 --> 00:02:40,025
That way, it kept for a few weeks.

64
00:02:40,475 --> 00:02:43,431
The system was put to the test at the Battle of

65
00:02:43,431 --> 00:02:45,318
Cambrai, on the Western Front.

66
00:02:45,318 --> 00:02:48,463
Not every soldier who received the blood survived.

67
00:02:48,463 --> 00:02:51,733
But by March 1918, American army hospitals in France

68
00:02:51,733 --> 00:02:54,375
had officially adopted Robertson's method.

69
00:02:54,375 --> 00:02:57,519
His depots became the model for the civilian blood

70
00:02:57,519 --> 00:02:58,903
banks that came after.

71
00:02:59,353 --> 00:03:03,347
According to one recent review, an estimated 10,000

72
00:03:03,347 --> 00:03:07,263
or more whole blood transfusions were performed in

73
00:03:07,263 --> 00:03:08,124
1918 alone.

74
00:03:08,924 --> 00:03:11,132
In the Second World War, the scale changed.

75
00:03:11,582 --> 00:03:15,225
By the end of the war, the military and the American

76
00:03:15,225 --> 00:03:18,727
Red Cross had collected more than 13 million pints

77
00:03:18,727 --> 00:03:19,778
of whole blood.

78
00:03:19,778 --> 00:03:23,421
In March 1945 alone, up to 62,000 units were shipped

79
00:03:23,421 --> 00:03:24,052
overseas.

80
00:03:24,502 --> 00:03:28,210
In 1945, an Army film showed whole blood from the

81
00:03:28,210 --> 00:03:31,161
home front, reaching soldiers overseas.

82
00:03:31,611 --> 00:03:34,896
Then came Korea, and the lesson had to be learned again.

83
00:03:34,896 --> 00:03:36,538
When the war began, the U.S.

84
00:03:36,538 --> 00:03:39,001
had no operational military blood program.

85
00:03:39,001 --> 00:03:41,934
The first blood shipments didn't arrive until more

86
00:03:41,934 --> 00:03:43,576
than two months into combat.

87
00:03:43,576 --> 00:03:46,568
Long supply lines and the short shelf life of whole

88
00:03:46,568 --> 00:03:49,618
blood led to significant waste, and drove innovation

89
00:03:49,618 --> 00:03:52,609
in plastic blood bags, and longer red cell storage.

90
00:03:53,059 --> 00:03:56,429
This Army film from December 1950 shows the Army's

91
00:03:56,429 --> 00:03:59,663
blood bank storage and shipment office in Tokyo,

92
00:03:59,663 --> 00:04:01,146
during the Korean War.

93
00:04:01,596 --> 00:04:04,825
In 1952, President Truman stood up what became the

94
00:04:04,825 --> 00:04:06,698
Armed Services Blood Program.

95
00:04:06,698 --> 00:04:09,862
After Korea, it took over collecting and shipping

96
00:04:09,862 --> 00:04:13,156
blood for the military from the American Red Cross.

97
00:04:13,606 --> 00:04:17,651
In Vietnam, whole blood was still at the center of care.

98
00:04:17,651 --> 00:04:21,335
Between 1965 and 1971, about 1.3 million units were

99
00:04:21,335 --> 00:04:22,346
shipped there.

100
00:04:22,346 --> 00:04:25,597
Roughly 600,000 were transfused, to more than

101
00:04:25,597 --> 00:04:28,414
330,000 American and allied casualties.

102
00:04:28,414 --> 00:04:32,098
And walking blood banks were part of that care too.

103
00:04:32,098 --> 00:04:33,687
Donors, close at hand.

104
00:04:34,137 --> 00:04:37,267
Then, after Vietnam, whole blood mostly disappeared.

105
00:04:37,717 --> 00:04:40,546
In the 1970s, the Army moved to what's called

106
00:04:40,546 --> 00:04:41,677
component therapy.

107
00:04:41,677 --> 00:04:44,381
Civilian medicine made the same move in the

108
00:04:44,381 --> 00:04:45,826
seventies and eighties.

109
00:04:46,276 --> 00:04:47,668
There were good reasons.

110
00:04:47,668 --> 00:04:50,394
Split into parts, one donation could help three

111
00:04:50,394 --> 00:04:51,786
patients instead of one.

112
00:04:51,786 --> 00:04:54,338
And components keep longer than whole blood.

113
00:04:54,788 --> 00:04:57,708
But a recent review argues that the switch was

114
00:04:57,708 --> 00:05:00,565
driven by economics, logistics, and perceived

115
00:05:00,565 --> 00:05:02,660
safety, not by clinical evidence.

116
00:05:02,660 --> 00:05:05,961
No prospective studies compared the two at the time.

117
00:05:06,411 --> 00:05:09,567
And for a medic far from a hospital, components had

118
00:05:09,567 --> 00:05:10,185
a problem.

119
00:05:10,185 --> 00:05:13,217
Plasma has to be kept frozen, and platelets bring

120
00:05:13,217 --> 00:05:14,454
their own logistics.

121
00:05:14,454 --> 00:05:17,548
An Army surgeon said components are not a feasible

122
00:05:17,548 --> 00:05:20,765
option for an immediate need for blood in the field.

123
00:05:21,215 --> 00:05:24,585
Robert Blough, a former Special Forces medical

124
00:05:24,585 --> 00:05:27,882
sergeant, remembers a man he treated in 2007.

125
00:05:27,882 --> 00:05:31,472
An Iraqi, shot in the lower abdomen, and bleeding

126
00:05:31,472 --> 00:05:32,277
internally.

127
00:05:32,727 --> 00:05:35,754
I did not have the ability to transfuse blood to

128
00:05:35,754 --> 00:05:36,636
save his life.

129
00:05:37,536 --> 00:05:40,351
So military medicine went back to the old idea.

130
00:05:40,801 --> 00:05:43,068
According to the review, the U.S.

131
00:05:43,068 --> 00:05:46,503
military re-adopted fresh whole blood from walking

132
00:05:46,503 --> 00:05:47,877
blood banks in 2002.

133
00:05:48,327 --> 00:05:51,723
A walking blood bank is, first of all, a list of names.

134
00:05:51,723 --> 00:05:54,933
Volunteers are tested ahead of time, for blood type,

135
00:05:54,933 --> 00:05:57,958
for antibody levels, and for diseases that can be

136
00:05:57,958 --> 00:05:59,501
passed on by transfusion.

137
00:05:59,501 --> 00:06:02,711
They're regularly retested for blood-borne diseases.

138
00:06:03,161 --> 00:06:05,750
When there's a casualty, and no stored blood, a

139
00:06:05,750 --> 00:06:08,174
donor from the list gives blood on the spot.

140
00:06:08,174 --> 00:06:11,038
It goes to the patient fresh, without refrigeration.

141
00:06:11,488 --> 00:06:14,391
A Navy surgeon explained why that matters.

142
00:06:14,391 --> 00:06:16,463
Stored blood may need warming.

143
00:06:16,463 --> 00:06:19,918
Fresh blood, he said, is already in our bodies, at

144
00:06:19,918 --> 00:06:20,817
98.6 degrees.

145
00:06:21,267 --> 00:06:24,408
In Valkyrie training, the Marine Corps says, blood

146
00:06:24,408 --> 00:06:28,051
drawn from a volunteer goes back into that same volunteer.

147
00:06:28,501 --> 00:06:30,379
Speed is part of the drill.

148
00:06:30,379 --> 00:06:33,925
At one 2026 Navy training event, the organizer said

149
00:06:33,925 --> 00:06:37,472
trained personnel must be able to screen donors and

150
00:06:37,472 --> 00:06:40,323
complete blood draws in under 28 minutes.

151
00:06:40,773 --> 00:06:44,338
In 2015, the Army's 75th Ranger Regiment began a

152
00:06:44,338 --> 00:06:47,011
program built on low titer O donors.

153
00:06:47,011 --> 00:06:48,496
They called it ROLO.

154
00:06:48,496 --> 00:06:49,907
Ranger O Low Titer.

155
00:06:49,907 --> 00:06:53,769
Using only low titer O donors cut out testing steps,

156
00:06:53,769 --> 00:06:57,631
and the chance of mismatching a donor and a patient.

157
00:06:58,081 --> 00:07:01,739
In May 2015, the first Ranger donors were prescreened.

158
00:07:01,739 --> 00:07:04,517
Months later, Rangers deployed with them.

159
00:07:04,517 --> 00:07:07,972
And in March 2016, the first shipment of stored low

160
00:07:07,972 --> 00:07:11,359
titer O whole blood left Joint Base Lewis-McChord,

161
00:07:11,359 --> 00:07:13,866
in Washington State, for Afghanistan.

162
00:07:14,316 --> 00:07:17,557
From concept to the unit level, it took 18 months.

163
00:07:17,557 --> 00:07:20,669
The regiment's surgeon, Lieutenant Colonel Ethan

164
00:07:20,669 --> 00:07:23,781
Miles, called the cleared donors a walking blood

165
00:07:23,781 --> 00:07:24,753
bank on target.

166
00:07:25,203 --> 00:07:28,614
In 2017, the program won one of the Army's Greatest

167
00:07:28,614 --> 00:07:29,818
Innovation Awards.

168
00:07:29,818 --> 00:07:33,295
That same year, a Navy surgeon, Lieutenant Commander

169
00:07:33,295 --> 00:07:36,639
Russell Wier, developed a version for corpsmen and

170
00:07:36,639 --> 00:07:38,846
Marines, inspired by the Rangers.

171
00:07:38,846 --> 00:07:40,250
It's called Valkyrie.

172
00:07:40,700 --> 00:07:43,967
In 2026, a Marine Corps unit hosted a Joint Trauma

173
00:07:43,967 --> 00:07:47,366
System version of the course, which the Marine Corps

174
00:07:47,366 --> 00:07:50,306
says implements a new Defense Department-wide

175
00:07:50,306 --> 00:07:53,116
standard for fresh whole blood transfusion.

176
00:07:53,116 --> 00:07:55,992
The five-day course runs from managing donor

177
00:07:55,992 --> 00:07:58,344
rosters, to performing transfusions.

178
00:07:58,794 --> 00:08:01,966
And stored low titer O whole blood came back too.

179
00:08:01,966 --> 00:08:04,878
The Rangers' first shipment went out in 2016.

180
00:08:04,878 --> 00:08:08,244
The review dates the military's reintroduction of it

181
00:08:08,244 --> 00:08:08,761
to 2017.

182
00:08:08,761 --> 00:08:11,933
Blood centers collect it from prescreened donors,

183
00:08:11,933 --> 00:08:14,263
refrigerate it, and ship it forward.

184
00:08:14,263 --> 00:08:17,111
By one Army Blood Program count, more than a

185
00:08:17,111 --> 00:08:19,117
thousand units had gone to U.S.

186
00:08:19,117 --> 00:08:20,153
Central Command.

187
00:08:20,153 --> 00:08:23,324
The Army Blood Program's director noted it can be

188
00:08:23,324 --> 00:08:25,395
kept in a standard refrigerator.

189
00:08:25,395 --> 00:08:28,696
And for teams close to the front, the program said,

190
00:08:28,696 --> 00:08:31,997
a unit of whole blood is often their only source of

191
00:08:31,997 --> 00:08:32,644
platelets.

192
00:08:33,094 --> 00:08:34,297
Why so much effort?

193
00:08:34,297 --> 00:08:36,829
A Navy lab chief put it in one sentence.

194
00:08:37,279 --> 00:08:40,345
One patient can wipe out an entire blood supply.

195
00:08:41,245 --> 00:08:43,281
The lesson didn't stay on the battlefield.

196
00:08:43,731 --> 00:08:47,136
At Mayo Clinic, doctors brought whole blood to air

197
00:08:47,136 --> 00:08:49,928
ambulances in southern Minnesota in 2015.

198
00:08:49,928 --> 00:08:53,468
In 2021, they began using it in children as young as

199
00:08:53,468 --> 00:08:54,081
4 months.

200
00:08:54,081 --> 00:08:57,622
Before a patient reaches the hospital, it travels in

201
00:08:57,622 --> 00:09:00,481
validated, temperature-controlled coolers.

202
00:09:00,931 --> 00:09:02,337
The reason is balance.

203
00:09:02,337 --> 00:09:05,467
In a massive transfusion, the goal is equal parts

204
00:09:05,467 --> 00:09:07,511
plasma, platelets and red cells.

205
00:09:07,511 --> 00:09:09,236
Whole blood comes that way.

206
00:09:09,236 --> 00:09:12,430
One Mayo specialist called it the perfect balanced

207
00:09:12,430 --> 00:09:14,219
resuscitation blood product.

208
00:09:14,669 --> 00:09:18,001
Mayo Clinic says about 15 to 20 percent of trauma

209
00:09:18,001 --> 00:09:20,042
patients receive transfusions.

210
00:09:20,042 --> 00:09:23,103
About 3 percent receive massive transfusions.

211
00:09:23,553 --> 00:09:26,099
Whole blood isn't the answer to everything.

212
00:09:26,099 --> 00:09:28,942
The review describes it as a first treatment for

213
00:09:28,942 --> 00:09:32,022
life-threatening bleeding, with a possible switch to

214
00:09:32,022 --> 00:09:35,043
type-specific components once the bleeding is under

215
00:09:35,043 --> 00:09:38,123
control, the patient is stable, or supplies run low.

216
00:09:38,573 --> 00:09:40,978
And it has a limit no lab has solved.

217
00:09:40,978 --> 00:09:42,799
Blood can't be manufactured.

218
00:09:42,799 --> 00:09:46,115
Every unit, whole or in parts, starts with a donor.

219
00:09:47,015 --> 00:09:49,924
So how do you get blood to someone who is bleeding,

220
00:09:49,924 --> 00:09:51,122
before it's too late?

221
00:09:51,572 --> 00:09:53,035
In 1917, an ice chest.

222
00:09:53,035 --> 00:09:56,692
In World War Two, millions of pints, collected at home.

223
00:09:56,692 --> 00:09:59,219
In Vietnam, more than a million units.

224
00:09:59,219 --> 00:10:00,282
Then, the parts.

225
00:10:00,282 --> 00:10:02,410
And now, the oldest idea of all.

226
00:10:02,860 --> 00:10:05,804
Robertson's ice chest became a list of names.

227
00:10:05,804 --> 00:10:09,140
People who agree, ahead of time, to give blood to a

228
00:10:09,140 --> 00:10:12,411
stranger on the worst day of that stranger's life.

229
00:10:12,861 --> 00:10:15,094
Next time on KEPT ALIVE. 1918.

230
00:10:15,094 --> 00:10:17,475
The flu that came home from war.

231
00:10:18,275 --> 00:10:19,416
For education only.

232
00:10:19,416 --> 00:10:21,036
This is not medical advice.

233
00:10:21,036 --> 00:10:23,858
If you have a health concern, talk to a doctor.
